<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rudolf I, Duke of Saxe-Wittenberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1284–<a href="/years/1356">1356</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rudolf I was duke of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> from 1298 to 1356. A close ally of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Charles IV</a>, he secured for his line the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> electoral vote and the office of imperial arch-marshal against the claims of the Saxe-Lauenburg branch. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a> confirmed the Wittenberg dukes as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-duke-of-saxe-wittenberg.jpg" alt="Rudolf I, Duke of Saxe-Wittenberg" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Cranach the Younger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rudolf_I_Herzog_von_Sachsen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rudolf, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ascania">House of Ascania</a>, was the eldest son of Duke Albert II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and Agnes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>, a daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf I of Germany</a>. In 1290 the king enfeoffed the young Rudolf with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> of the extinct counts of Brehna, and in 1296 the Ascanian lands were divided into the duchies of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> and Saxe-Lauenburg. Still a minor when his father died on 25 August 1298, he succeeded under his mother's regency and took up the rule himself in 1302.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Rudolf first had to make his Saxe-Lauenburg cousins agree that he, and not the eldest of them, John II, had inherited the electoral rank. In 1308 he voted for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-holy-roman-emperor">Henry of Luxembourg</a> and helped him with money and troops. In the double election of 1314 he voted for his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-fair">Frederick the Fair</a>, while John II, claiming the same vote, chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Louis the Bavarian</a>, who won at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhldorf">Battle of Mühldorf</a> in 1322. Louis then refused Rudolf the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, which he had administered as regent since 1319, and gave it with its electoral vote to his son Louis V.</p><p>Rudolf turned to mediation, organising the meeting of six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> behind the Declaration of Rhense in 1338. Under his mother's influence he began the expulsion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>, and he outlawed the Slavic languages of his territory. He founded the All Saints' Monastery and around 1340 built Wittenberg Castle. He was the only elector present at the coronation of Charles IV in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> on 26 November 1346, and received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altmark">Altmark</a> in 1347, which made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> the boundary between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull</a> made the electorates indivisible and inherited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a>, and the Saxe-Lauenburg branch lost all claim to the electoral vote and the office of Imperial Archmarshal. Rudolf died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> on 12 March 1356 and was succeeded by his eldest surviving son, Rudolf II. First buried in the Wittenberg Franciscan Church, his remains were moved to the All Saints' Church in 1883. The Saxe-Wittenberg line died out with his grandson Albert III in 1412, and its lands and electoral dignity passed to the Wettin margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rudolf I of Saxe-Wittenberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Rudolf I</b> was a close ally of Emperor Charles IV.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-wittenberg">Duchy of Saxe-Wittenberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Lutherstadt Wittenberg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.87,12.65&amp;year=1330" aria-label="Lutherstadt Wittenberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4835 370 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Rudolf I, Duke of Saxe-Wittenberg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5285 640h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5285 640h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.87,12.65&amp;year=1330">What was here in 1330? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
